entry mass-number
term: Mass Number
related_terms: isotope
related_terms: atomic-number
related_terms: proton
related_terms: neutron
related_terms: nucleus
quick_facts: Mass number = protons + neutrons
quick_facts: It is written after the name, as in uranium-235
quick_facts: Neutrons = mass number minus atomic number
quick_facts: Isotopes of one element have different mass numbers
quick_facts: Electrons weigh so little that they are not counted

sources:
    {"organization":"OpenStax","title":"Chemistry 2e: Atomic Structure and Symbolism","url":"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism"}

definition:
    The total number of protons and neutrons in a nucleus. It is the number written after an element's name: carbon-14 has a mass number of 14. To find the neutrons, take away the protons. Carbon has 6 protons, so carbon-14 has 8 neutrons. The mass number is not the same as the atomic number, which counts only protons.

debunking_snippet:
    “The number in carbon-14 is the number of neutrons.” It counts protons and neutrons together. Carbon always has 6 protons, so carbon-14 has 8 neutrons.
